{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JzEiNdt4ZT8Ha6guLGLRrw","type":"tool_result","content":"12:- [Why does emoji break alignment in a Table or Panel?](#why-does-emoji-break-alignment-in-a-table-or-panel)\n13-\n14-<a name=\"extra-space-not-enough-space-in-jupyter-output\"></a>\n15-## Extra space, not enough space, in Jupyter output\n16-\n17-There are many different implementations of Jupyter, from different vendors.\n18-\n19-Different notebook software may render Rich's output differently, due to how the CSS is constructed.\n20-Adding or removing space, may make the output look good on your software, but break somewhere else.\n21-\n22-I have been adding and removing new lines for jupyter since Rich added support, and I am reluctant to continue to do that, *unless* there is some supporting evidence that Rich is doing the wrong thing.\n23-I'm afraid that making it look better for your software isn't evidence.\n24-\n--\n76:Some terminals don't display emoji with the correct width, which means Rich can't always align them accurately inside a panel.\n77-\n78-<a name=\"rich-is-automatically-installing-traceback-handler\"></a>\n79-## Rich is automatically installing traceback handler.\n80-\n81-Rich will never install the traceback handler automatically.\n82-\n83-If you are getting Rich tracebacks and you don't want them, then some other piece of software is calling `rich.traceback.install()`.\n84-\n85-<a name=\"strange-colors-in-console-output\"></a>\n86-## Strange colors in console output.\n87-\n88-Rich will highlight certain patterns in your output such as numbers, strings, and other objects like IP addresses.\n--\n100:<a name=\"why-does-emoji-break-alignment-in-a-table-or-panel\"></a>\n101:## Why does emoji break alignment in a Table or Panel?\n102-\n103:Certain emoji take up double space within the terminal. Unfortunately, terminals don't always agree how wide a given character should be.\n104-\n105-Rich has no way of knowing how wide a character will be on any given terminal. This can break alignment in containers like Table and Panel, where Rich needs to know the width of the content.\n106-\n107:There are also *multiple codepoints* characters, such as country flags, and emoji modifiers, which produce wildly different results across terminal emulators.\n108-\n109:Fortunately, most characters will work just fine. But you may have to avoid using the emojis that break alignment. You will get good results if you stick to emoji released on or before version 9 of the Unicode database,\n110-\n111-<hr>\n112-\n113-Generated by [FAQtory](https://github.com/willmcgugan/faqtory)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5aaa88ce-b3c1-40c0-8b34-15346b0c79e5","uuid":"f35079cf-655a-4565-a04a-458d7ae23d23","timestamp":"2026-10-27T08:02:44.969Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"12:- [Why does emoji break alignment in a Table or Panel?](#why-does-emoji-break-alignment-in-a-table-or-panel)\n13-\n14-<a name=\"extra-space-not-enough-space-in-jupyter-output\"></a>\n15-## Extra space, not enough space, in Jupyter output\n16-\n17-There are many different implementations of Jupyter, from different vendors.\n18-\n19-Different notebook software may render Rich's output differently, due to how the CSS is constructed.\n20-Adding or removing space, may make the output look good on your software, but break somewhere else.\n21-\n22-I have been adding and removing new lines for jupyter since Rich added support, and I am reluctant to continue to do that, *unless* there is some supporting evidence that Rich is doing the wrong thing.\n23-I'm afraid that making it look better for your software isn't evidence.\n24-\n--\n76:Some terminals don't display emoji with the correct width, which means Rich can't always align them accurately inside a panel.\n77-\n78-<a name=\"rich-is-automatically-installing-traceback-handler\"></a>\n79-## Rich is automatically installing traceback handler.\n80-\n81-Rich will never install the traceback handler automatically.\n82-\n83-If you are getting Rich tracebacks and you don't want them, then some other piece of software is calling `rich.traceback.install()`.\n84-\n85-<a name=\"strange-colors-in-console-output\"></a>\n86-## Strange colors in console output.\n87-\n88-Rich will highlight certain patterns in your output such as numbers, strings, and other objects like IP addresses.\n--\n100:<a name=\"why-does-emoji-break-alignment-in-a-table-or-panel\"></a>\n101:## Why does emoji break alignment in a Table or Panel?\n102-\n103:Certain emoji take up double space within the terminal. Unfortunately, terminals don't always agree how wide a given character should be.\n104-\n105-Rich has no way of knowing how wide a character will be on any given terminal. This can break alignment in containers like Table and Panel, where Rich needs to know the width of the content.\n106-\n107:There are also *multiple codepoints* characters, such as country flags, and emoji modifiers, which produce wildly different results across terminal emulators.\n108-\n109:Fortunately, most characters will work just fine. But you may have to avoid using the emojis that break alignment. You will get good results if you stick to emoji released on or before version 9 of the Unicode database,\n110-\n111-<hr>\n112-\n113-Generated by [FAQtory](https://github.com/willmcgugan/faqtory)","numLines":42,"totalLines":42},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JzEiNdt4ZT8Ha6guLGLRrw","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
